    <h2>What this page could not verify<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <div class="warn">
      Four things, stated plainly so you know which parts of this page are reads and which are reconstruction.
      <br><br>
      <b>1. The exit passcode and the three face-down cards.</b> The keypad is on camera twice (5:02 and 5:32) and the entry is masked both times, so there is no digit string to print. The card table is readable as a shape — fifteen cards, three rows of five, one face-down per row, diamonds/spades/hearts — but the face-up ranks changed between our two independent reads (the bottom row came back as 3-4-5-?-7 once and A-3-5-?-7 another time), which means any code derived from them would be a guess wearing a number's clothes. So the page gives you the method and not the answer.
      <br><br>
      <b>2. Where the piece of tape goes.</b> The roll and the scissors are on the pegboard, the combine happens at 4:02, and the panel refuses a tap at 4:20 with <b>It's too dangerous right now!</b> — but no frame in the 346 we pulled shows tape stuck to a cable, a terminal or a door. We are not going to print "tape the exposed wire" as a step when the evidence is a two-second gap.
      <br><br>
      <b>3. The name of the yellow part from the tool chest drawer.</b> The drawer's close-up shows a coiled black cable, and the inventory slot that appears afterwards is a yellow channel-shaped part that reads as a stacked pair — but the game never shows a pickup label for it, so this page describes it rather than naming it.
      <br><br>
      <b>4. What the two yellow-handled breakers do.</b> Two large breakers with yellow handles are clearly there, and the panel's red/green indicator pair is readable. What is not readable is whether either breaker is interactive: the run never taps them, and the panel's power state changes when the tile board is solved, not when a breaker is thrown. Their handle positions and their printed labels (C32, with a DK marking) also changed between our two reads of the same panel, along with the miniature-breaker count (10 vs 12) and the energy meter's digits — so none of those are printed as facts.
    </div>
